msgid "Use"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:76
msgid "the value"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:77
msgid "the index, and you need the item"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:77
msgid "`item = lst.pop(index)` (`lst.pop()`: the last one)"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:78
msgid "the index, or a slice"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:80
msgid "These change the list **in place** - there is no new list. In SQL it is a `DELETE` on the table itself; in SAS you would write a new data set without the rows."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:83
msgid "`remove`: only the first one, and an error if it is not there"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:85
msgid "`remove` removes **one** item - the first one equal to the value. When the value is not in the list, it stops with a `ValueError`. Test with `in` first ([session 3](./03-lists-tuples-sets.md#membership-in-not-in)):"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:102
msgid "To remove **all** items with one value, build a new list without them - the list comprehension of [section 2](#_2-list-comprehensions)."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:105
msgid "`clear`: empty the list"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:115
msgid "A common use: collect items in a loop, process them in groups, and empty the list after each group."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:118
msgid "`index`: the position of an item"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:120
msgid "`lst.index(value)` gives the index of the **first** item equal to the value - and, like `remove`, a `ValueError` when there is none:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:133
msgid "With two lists in the same order, `index` finds the position in one list, and the same position reads the other. (Section 4 shows `zip`, which walks over both lists together.)"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:137
msgid "`lst.count(value)` ([session 3](./03-lists-tuples-sets.md#len-min-max-sum-count-index)) says how many times the value is there; `count(x) > 0` is the same as `x in lst`."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:140 site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:485 site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:599 site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:723
msgid "Try it"
msgstr ""

