#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:146
msgid "Sort a list: `sort()` and `sorted()`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:148
msgid "`lst.sort()` sorts **this** list, in place. Only lists have it."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:149
msgid "`sorted(x)` returns a **new**, sorted list and does not change `x`. It works on any sequence: a list, a tuple, a set, a string."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:151
msgid "Both take `reverse=True`: from the largest."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:163
msgid "Text sorts alphabetically, capital letters before small ones ([session 2](./02-conditions-and-loops.md#strings-are-compared-letter-by-letter)):"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:170
msgid "In SAS this is `PROC SORT` (it sorts the data set in place, like `sort()`, unless you give `OUT=`); in SQL `ORDER BY` (it returns new rows, like `sorted()`)."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:173
msgid "The methods return `None`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:174
msgid "`sort`, `append`, `remove`, `clear` and the other methods change the list and return `None`, not the list. A classic bug:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:183
msgid "`amounts.sort()` sorts the list and returns `None`, and `amounts = ...` keeps that `None` - the list is lost. Write only `amounts.sort()`, or `amounts = sorted(amounts)`."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:188
msgid "Sort by a key"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:190
msgid "[Session 4](./04-functions.md#sorting-by-a-key) showed `key=`: a function that says **what** to compare. With records - tuples or lists - the key picks the column:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:210
msgid "`max` and `min` take the same `key=`, and give back the **whole record**:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:223
msgid "The top N"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:225
msgid "Sort from the largest, then take a slice ([session 3](./03-lists-tuples-sets.md#slices-start-stop-step)): `sorted(...)[:n]` is SQL `ORDER BY ... DESC FETCH FIRST n ROWS ONLY`, or in SAS `PROC SORT` with `BY DESCENDING`, then `SET sorted(OBS=n)`."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:239
msgid "The fields of a file are text: `float(row[7])` in the key compares the amounts as numbers. Without it, `\"9\"` would be larger than `\"10\"`."
