#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:282
msgid "2. List comprehensions"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:284
msgid "From a loop to one line"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:286
msgid "In sessions 2 and 3 you built lists with a loop and `append`:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:298
msgid "This pattern - an empty list, a loop, one `append` - is so common that Python has a short form for it, the **list comprehension**:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:308
msgid "Read it from the middle: \"**for** each `amount` **in** `amounts`, take `amount / 1000`\". The square brackets say: collect the results in a new list."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:317
msgid "It is the same as `SELECT amount / 1000 FROM amounts` in SQL, and the same as a derived variable in a SAS DATA step:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:327
msgid "The original list does not change: a comprehension always makes a **new** list."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:329
msgid "Any expression"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:331
msgid "The expression can be anything that gives a value: a calculation, a method call, a function call, a tuple."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:344
msgid "With your own function from [session 4](./04-functions.md) - a `CASE WHEN` with many branches, written once and used in one short line:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:361
msgid "Filter with `if`: the `WHERE`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:363
msgid "Add `if condition` at the end, and only the items that pass go into the new list:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:380
msgid "In SQL: `SELECT d FROM days WHERE d > 90`; in SAS: a subsetting `IF` in a DATA step. With both parts - a new value **and** a filter:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:390
msgid "This is also the safe way to \"remove\" items (the warning of section 1): keep the ones you want, in a new list."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:399
msgid "On the portfolio"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:401
msgid "Read `loans.csv` into a list of rows - one comprehension - and select from it. Each row is a list of strings, in the order of the header. The ids of the stage 3 loans (`stage` is column 10), and the outstanding amounts in thousands of the USD loans:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:421
msgid "The columns are still **text** - `row[10] == \"3\"`, not `== 3`. Convert where you need a number (`float(row[7])`), as in [session 2](./02-conditions-and-loops.md#the-rows-of-a-file). The position of a column is `header.index(\"stage\")` (section 1)."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:425
msgid "`CASE WHEN` inside: `a if condition else b`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:427
msgid "A conditional expression ([session 2](./02-conditions-and-loops.md#_7-conditional-expressions)) can be the expression of a comprehension. Then every item goes into the list, with one of two values:"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:437
msgid "The position of `if` decides what it does"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:438
msgid "`if` **at the end** filters: fewer items. No `else` is allowed there."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:439
msgid "`a if c else b` **at the start** chooses a value: the same number of items. The `else` is required."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:455
msgid "With curly brackets instead of square ones, you get a **set** - the distinct values ([session 3](./03-lists-tuples-sets.md#unique-values-sql-select-distinct)): `{row[2] for row in rows}` is `SELECT DISTINCT product`."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:459
msgid "When not to use a comprehension"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:461
msgid "A comprehension is for **building a new list**. Use a normal loop when:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:463
msgid "you only want an effect, not a list - for example printing;"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:464
msgid "the logic needs several steps or several `if` / `elif` branches - put it into a function and call the function in the comprehension, as with `dpd_bucket` above, or write the loop;"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:467
msgid "the line becomes too long to read in one look (more than about 80 characters)."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:470
msgid "In pandas ([session 14](./14-numpy-pandas-intro.md#columns)) you do not loop at all - an operation on a column works on all its values:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:481
msgid "The idea is the same as a comprehension: a new column from an old one, with a filter."
msgstr ""

