#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:498
msgid "3. Generator expressions: no list in memory"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:500
msgid "`sum(... for ...)`: SUM and COUNT with a condition"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:502
msgid "When you only need the **total**, you do not need the list. Write the comprehension without the square brackets, inside the call:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:517
msgid "`sum(x for ... if ...)` is SQL `SUM(x) ... WHERE ...`;"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:518
msgid "`sum(1 for ... if ...)` is SQL `COUNT(*) ... WHERE ...`: one `1` for each row that passes."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:521
msgid "This is a **generator expression**. It works inside any function that takes many values: `sum`, `min`, `max`, `sorted`, `list`, `\", \".join(...)`."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:524
msgid "Why: a generator does not keep the values"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:526
msgid "A list comprehension builds the **whole list** first, and keeps all its values in memory. A generator expression keeps **none** of them: it makes the next value only when `sum` asks for it, gives it, and forgets it."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:541
msgid "The result is the same; the memory is not. (On your computer the list takes about 8 MB; in the browser of this site about 4 MB. The numbers inside it take more memory again.) It is the idea of `range` in [session 3](./03-lists-tuples-sets.md#a-range-does-not-keep-its-numbers): a range of a billion numbers is small, because it does not keep them. A SAS DATA step works this way too: it reads one observation, processes it, and goes to the next."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:548
msgid "(The round brackets make a generator on its own line. Inside a call - `sum(x for x in ...)` - the brackets of the call are enough.)"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:551
msgid "A file, one line at a time"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:553
msgid "A file works the same way: `for line in file` reads **one line at a time**. A generator over the file gives the total of a file of any size, and only one line is in memory at a time:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:565
msgid "The list of rows of section 2 (`rows = [... for line in file]`) keeps the whole file in memory. For `loans.csv` (400 rows) both are fine. For a file of 10 million rows, the generator still works on a laptop."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:570
msgid "`pd.read_csv` reads the **whole** file into memory. For a file larger than the memory, `chunksize=` reads it in pieces, and you add up the pieces - the same idea:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:583
msgid "A trap: a generator is used once"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:585
msgid "A generator gives its values **once**. After that it is empty - there is nothing kept to give again:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:595
msgid "It also has no `len()` and no index (`late[0]` is an error). When you need the values more than once, or their number, make a **list** - that is what lists are for."
msgstr ""

#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:612
msgid "4. `zip` and `enumerate`"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:614
msgid "`zip`: several lists side by side"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:616
msgid "`zip` takes several lists and gives **tuples**: the first items together, then the second items, and so on:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:630
msgid "Each item of `zip` is a tuple, and `for loan_id, product, amount in ...` takes it apart into three names - the **unpacking** of [session 3](./03-lists-tuples-sets.md#unpacking), `loan_id, product, amount = (\"L000001\", \"investment\", 1044231.30)`, done for every item."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:635
msgid "Columns become rows. A common use: two lists of the same months, compared month by month:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:647
msgid "And in a comprehension - a new list from two lists, item by item:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:657
msgid "`zip` gives its pairs one by one, like a generator: `list(zip(...))` shows them all. Two lists - the keys and the values - become a dictionary with `dict(zip(keys, values))` in [session 6](./06-dictionaries.md)."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:661
msgid "A trap: the shortest list wins"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:663
msgid "`zip` stops at the end of the **shortest** list - without an error. A missing value in one list silently drops the last row:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:673
msgid "Check the lengths first when the lists come from different places: `len(loan_ids) == len(amounts)`."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:676
msgid "`enumerate`: the number of each item"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:678
msgid "Sometimes you need the **position** of each item, not only the item. [`enumerate(items, start=0)`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#enumerate) gives pairs `(number, item)` - it is a `zip` of the numbers 0, 1, 2, ... and the items:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:692
msgid "With `start=1` the number is a **rank** - here for the top N of section 1:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:707
msgid "In SAS, the number of the current row in a DATA step is `_N_`; in SQL, a rank is `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY outstanding DESC)`."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/05-lists-advanced.md:711
msgid "In a DataFrame, the columns are already side by side - no `zip` is needed. A new column from two columns is one line:"
msgstr ""

